  "conclusion": "FALSIFIED. No tested regime gives PARI a consistent advantage: balanced orbits 1.00x (D3), skewed orbits 0.91-1.09x (D1), informative orbits 0.83-0.94x (D4, PARI worse). D4 does show the expected bias mechanism (PARI bias ~+0.001 vs POOLED ~-0.010) but pairing pays for it with larger variance, so RMSE is worse. Under this DGP family the patient-anchored estimator is NOT a better estimator of a global temperature than a simple pooled histogram matcher. Per the registered failure clause, PARI as specified must be DOWNGRADED / abandoned; it cannot be the novelty claim of the T1 paper. The surviving empirical finding is E5: group heterogeneity makes a single global T untransferable (regret 0 -> 0.156), which supports the PROBLEM, not this solution."
